{
  "topic": "proper fractions",
  "affirmation": "You taught fractions with warm, concrete examples that students could picture.",
  "identity": "You teach by showing — you bring circles, squares, sets of colored objects and a cake story into the room so the idea of a ‘part’ becomes something students can hold in their heads. You speak in a calm, inviting way (\"ایک منٹ، ایک منٹ، ایک منٹ۔ آپ بولیں\") and you step in quickly to correct and name terms so the lesson keeps moving.",
  "moments": [
    {
      "title": "Confused description",
      "quote": "4۔ اوپر، نیچے، اور اوپر، اوپر آئے گا",
      "why": "This line mattered because it showed a student trying to explain what a shaded part is, which gave you a real window into their thinking."
    },
    {
      "title": "Test-score example",
      "quote": "10 میں سے 9، 9، 10 میں سے 1 گئے تو 9 بچ گیا۔ 9 اور 10 نیچے، اور 9 اوپر.",
      "why": "This mattered because a student attempted a real-life link to fractions (test scores), revealing a mix of subtraction and fraction ideas you could build from."
    },
    {
      "title": "Numerator placement",
      "quote": "میم، میم، میم، اوپر آئے گا، اور اوپر 5 آئے گا",
      "why": "This mattered because it captured a persistent uncertainty about where numbers go in a fraction — a direct cue about what to clarify next."
    }
  ],
  "strength_name": "Clear content explanations",
  "strength_note": "You clearly named numerator and denominator, used the cake analogy and concrete models, and corrected counts aloud so students left with the right vocabulary.",
  "horizon_title": "Two task lanes",
  "horizon_note": "Adding two task lanes — a simpler prompt and a slightly harder prompt — would let you keep your whole-class clarity while giving both struggling and faster students something at their level.",
  "journey_note": "In this 16‑minute lesson you reached 68%: your dependable use of models and clear terminology carried the class, and the same teaching pattern kept appearing.",
  "score_framing": "A 68% score is a real milestone — it shows strong, repeatable moves you can sharpen into even more student thinking.",
  "domain_whys": {
    "high_leverage_practices": "This is developing because you directed the procedure and gave the same counting-and-labeling task to the whole class (for example when students counted shaded parts on the circle and square models) — it's not full marks because you did not invite students to choose or show different solution methods or present their own approaches.",
    "student_engagement": "This is strong because students answered and echoed your prompts and life examples (for example one student said 'جو کلر کیا ہوا ہے، وہ اوپر ہے، اور ٹوٹل نیچے ہے.') — it's not full marks because most replies were short echoes and there were no student-initiated content questions about fractions.",
    "teacher_subject_knowledge": "This is strong because you named terms and corrected errors (for example when a student answered '6' for a four-part square you counted '1،2،3،4' aloud and fixed the record) — it's not full marks because you did not explicitly name or unpack the common misconceptions students showed and you accepted short answers without pressing for their reasoning."
  }
}
